var ht = `Claude's workspace runtime failed to start with errno -4094 (spawn UNKNOWN).

This is usually WDAC, AppLocker, or antivirus/endpoint protection blocking execution of the runtime binary, which is downloaded under the user profile:

  %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Claude-3p\\claude-code\\<version>\\claude.exe   (third-party deployment)
  %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude-code\\<version>\\claude.exe           (standard deployment)

The runtime also spawns ripgrep (rg.exe) from the same directory.

To confirm which control is blocking it, check Event Viewer:
  - WDAC:       Applications and Services Logs > Microsoft > Windows > CodeIntegrity > Operational, Event ID 3077
  - AppLocker:  Applications and Services Logs > Microsoft > Windows > AppLocker > EXE and DLL, Event ID 8004
  - Defender:   Applications and Services Logs > Microsoft > Windows > Windows Defender > Operational, Event ID 1116 / 1117

To fix, add a publisher rule for "Anthropic, PBC" to the WDAC or AppLocker policy applied to this device. The binary is code-signed (verify with: Get-AuthenticodeSignature on claude.exe), so a publisher rule covers all install modes and version updates.

If publisher rules aren't available, add a path rule instead:
  - AppLocker (3P):  %OSDRIVE%\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Claude-3p\\claude-code\\*
  - AppLocker (1P):  %OSDRIVE%\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\Claude\\claude-code\\*
  - WDAC (3P):       %OSDRIVE%\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Claude-3p\\claude-code\\*
  - WDAC (1P):       %OSDRIVE%\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\Claude\\claude-code\\*
    Note: WDAC ignores FilePath rules to user-writable locations unless the policy sets "Disabled:Runtime FilePath Rule Protection".

If Defender or other antivirus is blocking it, add an exclusion for the directory above or allow the detected item.`;
var gt = `Claude (third-party deployment) stopped receiving data from the inference gateway part-way through a /v1/messages response. The gateway had returned response headers, and usually part of the body; then no bytes arrived within the client's stream idle timeout (five minutes unless the deployment overrides it) and the client closed the connection (CLI error "stream idle: no bytes for \u2026ms", "Stream idle timeout", or "The operation timed out").

This happens while the model is working without emitting output \u2014 extended thinking, or generating a large tool input such as a long file write \u2014 and nothing on the path sends keep-alive data. Amazon Bedrock's event stream has no keep-alive frames of its own, so a gateway that translates from Bedrock must add them.

To fix, on the gateway:
  - Emit SSE keep-alives ("event: ping" frames or ": comment" lines) on streaming responses while waiting on the model, well inside that timeout \u2014 at least once a minute. LiteLLM 1.97 or later does this by default on /v1/messages.
  - Make sure every proxy or load balancer between the gateway and this device passes streaming responses through unbuffered and has no idle timeout shorter than the keep-alive interval.

The app's diagnostic report includes a Claude Code diagnostics log, where this appears as a cli_stream_failed entry.`;
var _t = `Claude (third-party deployment) got no complete response from the inference gateway on any attempt, including the automatic retries: each one hit the client's timeout, about five minutes without data (CLI error "Request timed out").

Common causes:
  - The gateway, or a proxy in front of it, buffers the model's streaming response or waits on the model without sending anything, so a long generation never delivers a byte in time. The gateway must stream /v1/messages responses through as they are produced and emit SSE keep-alives ("event: ping" frames or ": comment" lines) at least once a minute while the model is working; an upstream such as Amazon Bedrock sends none of its own. LiteLLM 1.97 or later does this by default on /v1/messages.
  - The gateway is overloaded or hung, or something between this device and the gateway (proxy, firewall, VPN) accepts the connection but never returns a response.

Check the gateway's health and its logs for these requests first. The app's diagnostic report includes a Claude Code diagnostics log; the retries appear as cli_api_retry entries.`;
